<section aria-labelledby="fcc-sg-heading-template--22974334501083__fcc_stage_guide_WfEUcT" class="fcc-sg" id="fcc-sg-template--22974334501083__fcc_stage_guide_WfEUcT">
<div class="fcc-sg__wrap">
<div class="fcc-sg__left"><p class="fcc-sg__subheading">HOW TO CHOOSE</p><h2 class="fcc-sg__heading" id="fcc-sg-heading-template--22974334501083__fcc_stage_guide_WfEUcT">The Right Organic Baby Formula Stage or Specialty Formula For My Baby</h2>
<p class="fcc-sg__desc">HiPP stages correspond to your baby’s age and developmental needs. Here’s a simple guide to help you choose:</p>
</div>
<div class="fcc-sg__right">
<div class="fcc-sg__table-wrapper">
<table class="fcc-sg__table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Stage</th>
<th scope="col">Key Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>
<span class="fcc-sg__stage-title">Stage PRE</span><span class="fcc-sg__stage-age">(From birth)</span></td>
<td>
<p class="fcc-sg__key-notes">Lactose only as carbohydrate; no added starch; suitable for premature babies</p>
</td>
</tr><tr>
<td>
<span class="fcc-sg__stage-title">Stage 1</span><span class="fcc-sg__stage-age">(From birth)</span></td>
<td>
<p class="fcc-sg__key-notes">Similar to PRE; may contain small amounts of starch for added satiation; may continue beyond 6 months if your baby is thriving</p>
</td>
</tr><tr>
<td>
<span class="fcc-sg__stage-title">Stage 2</span><span class="fcc-sg__stage-age">(6 months+)</span></td>
<td>
<p class="fcc-sg__key-notes">Designed to anticipate complementary foods being introduced</p>
</td>
</tr><tr>
<td>
<span class="fcc-sg__stage-title">Stage 3</span><span class="fcc-sg__stage-age">(10 months+)</span></td>
<td>
<p class="fcc-sg__key-notes">Supports transition toward a more varied diet</p>
</td>
</tr><tr>
<td>
<span class="fcc-sg__stage-title">Kindermilch</span><span class="fcc-sg__stage-age">(12 months+)</span></td>
<td>
<p class="fcc-sg__key-notes">Growing-up milk for toddlers</p>
</td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div><p class="fcc-sg__footer">If your baby is doing well on their current formula, there’s generally no need to change stages early, unless advised otherwise by your pediatrician or healthcare provider. When in doubt, your pediatrician can help guide the transition.</p></div>
</div>
